            <p class="monograph-callout-text">
              These profiles show that <strong>each source has a completely individual face</strong>. It is unlikely that a forger ordering materials and equipping them with false labels would create units so strongly differentiated from each other. At the same time, it is to be noted that the profiles only represent very rough outlines and that the differences are reinforced as one goes into greater detail, for instance, inquiring into the geographical affiliations of the sources or the formal characteristics of the texts. Thus, analysis of the structure of transmission of the <em>Muṣannaf</em> of ʿAbd al-Razzāq and his main sources leads to the conclusion that the evidence more probably reflects real sources than fictions of ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s.
            </p>

            <p>Some further formal characteristics which are conspicuous in ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s presentation of the traditions point in the same direction,<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn28" id="fnref28" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="28" data-fn-text="The term “tradition” is used, in addition to its common meaning, as a synonym for ḥadīth, athar or khabar." aria-label="Footnote 28: I use the term “tradition,” in addition to its common meanin">28</a></sup> for instance, the fact that ʿAbd al-Razzāq occasionally expresses his uncertainty about the exact origin of a tradition. An example:
            </p>

            <div class="monograph-isnād-extract">
              <div class="extract-label">Example of Expressed Teacher Uncertainty (Shakk)</div>
              <blockquote class="extract-body">
                ʿAbd al-Razzāq from al-Thawrī from Mughīra or someone else—<strong>Abū Bakr [i.e., ʿAbd al-Razzāq] was unsure about it</strong>—from Ibrāhīm, who said: . . .<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn29" id="fnref29" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="29" data-fn-text="AM 6: 11825 (The number before the colon indicates the volume; the number after it is always the number of the text)." aria-label="Footnote 29: AM 6: 11825 (The number before the colon indicates the volum">29</a></sup>
              </blockquote>
            </div>

            <p>
              In the case of a notorious forger such doubts are scarcely to be expected, because they would compromise his actual aim, the feigning of certain and unbroken transmission.
            </p>

            <p>
              ʿAbd al-Razzāq claims to have received thousands of texts directly from Ibn Jurayj, al-Thawrī and Maʿmar. This could be a fabrication. However, the fact that, for instance, isnāds such as:
            </p>

            <div class="monograph-chain-display">
              <div class="chain-item">
                <span class="chain-arrow">➔</span>
                <code>ʿAbd al-Razzāq ➔ al-Thawrī ➔ Ibn Jurayj . . .</code>
                <span class="chain-ref"><sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn30" id="fnref30" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="30" data-fn-text="Cf. AM 6: 11682; 7: 12631, 13020, 13607." aria-label="Footnote 30: Cf. AM 6: 11682; 7: 12631, 13020, 13607.">30</a></sup></span>
              </div>
              <div class="chain-item">
                <span class="chain-arrow">➔</span>
                <code>ʿAbd al-Razzāq ➔ Ibn Jurayj ➔ al-Thawrī . . .</code>
                <span class="chain-ref"><sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn31" id="fnref31" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="31" data-fn-text="Cf. AM 6: 10984." aria-label="Footnote 31: Cf. AM 6: 10984.">31</a></sup></span>
              </div>
              <div class="chain-item">
                <span class="chain-arrow">➔</span>
                <code>ʿAbd al-Razzāq ➔ al-Thawrī ➔ Maʿmar . . .</code>
                <span class="chain-ref"><sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn32" id="fnref32" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="32" data-fn-text="Cf. AM 6: 10798." aria-label="Footnote 32: Cf. AM 6: 10798.">32</a></sup></span>
              </div>
            </div>

            <p class="is-continuation">
              appear, and thus that indirect transmission from his main informants also occurs, is an indicator that ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s statements about origins are not arbitrarily chosen but really designate the sources from which the relevant traditions derive. This fact is just as irreconcilable with the forgery theory as ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s transmitting anonymous reports from people for whom he otherwise names one of his main sources, for instance, <em>ʿAbd al-Razzāq from a Medinan scholar (shaykh), who said: I heard Ibn Shihāb report from . . .</em>, or <em>ʿAbd al-Razzāq from someone (rajul) from Ḥammād from . . .</em><sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn33" id="fnref33" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="33" data-fn-text="Cf. AM 7: 12795, 13622." aria-label="Footnote 33: Cf. AM 7: 12795, 13622.">33</a></sup> In general, he has traditions of Ibn Shihāb from Ibn Jurayj or Maʿmar and texts of Ḥammād from al-Thawrī or Maʿmar.
            </p>

            <p>
              The results obtained from within the work find confirmation through reports of biographical character contained in various later works. Separate evaluation of these sources is advisable for methodological reasons, because the authenticity of the biographical traditions is no less controversial than that of the ḥadīth and the early legal traditions.
            </p>
          </section>
          <section id="section-b" class="monograph-section">
            <h2 class="section-heading">
              <span class="heading-roman">IV.</span>
              <span class="heading-title">B. The Author and His Work in Light of Biographical Sources</span>
            </h2>

            <p>According to the biographical literature, his full name is Abū Bakr ʿAbd al-Razzāq ibn Hammām ibn Nāfiʿ.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn34" id="fnref34" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="34" data-fn-text="Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt , vol. 5, p. 399. Khalīfa ibn Khayyāṭ, Ṭabaqāt , p. 289. al-Bukhārī, Taʾrīkh , vol. 3/2, p. 130. Ibn Abī Ḥātim, Jarḥ , vol. 3, p. 38. Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh , vol. 36, p. 160. Ibn Khallikān, Wafayāt , vol. 2, p. 371. Ibn al-Nadīm, Fihrist , p. 318. al-Dhahabī, Tadhkira , vol. 1, p. 364. Id., Mīzān , vol. 2, p. 126. al-Ṣafadī, Nakt , p. 191. Ibn Ḥajar, Tahdhīb , vol. 6, p. 310." aria-label="Footnote 34: Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt , vol. 5, p. 399. Khalīfa ibn Khayyāṭ, Ṭab">34</a></sup> As <em>nisba</em>s we find: al-Ṣanʿānī,<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn35" id="fnref35" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="35" data-fn-text="Ibn Abī Ḥātim, Jarḥ , vol. 3, p. 38 and the literature following Ibn Abī Ḥātim in the preceding note." aria-label="Footnote 35: Ibn Abī Ḥātim, Jarḥ , vol. 3, p. 38 and the literature follo">35</a></sup> al-Yamānī<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn36" id="fnref36" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="36" data-fn-text="al-Bukhārī, Taʾrīkh , vol. 3/2, p. 130. Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh , vol. 36, pp. 164, 165." aria-label="Footnote 36: al-Bukhārī, Taʾrīkh , vol. 3/2, p. 130. Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh">36</a></sup> and al-Ḥimyarī.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn37" id="fnref37" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="37" data-fn-text="Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh , vol. 36, p. 160. al-Dhahabī, Tadhkira , vol. 1, p. 364. Id., Mīzān , vol. 2, p. 126. al-Ṣafadī, Nakt , p. 191. Ibn Ḥajar, Tahdhīb , vol. 6, p. 310." aria-label="Footnote 37: Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh , vol. 36, p. 160. al-Dhahabī, Tadhkira">37</a></sup> The last should indicate that he was a <em>mawlā</em> of the Ḥimyar.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn38" id="fnref38" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="38" data-fn-text="See note 34 and Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh , vol. 36, pp. 164, 166." aria-label="Footnote 38: See note 34 and Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh , vol. 36, pp. 164, 166">38</a></sup> Born in the year 126/744,<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn39" id="fnref39" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="39" data-fn-text="Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh , vol. 36, p. 163. Ibn Khallikān, Wafayāt , vol. 2, p. 371. al-Dhahabī, Mīzān , vol. 2, p. 126. al-Ṣafadī, Nakt , p. 191. Ibn Ḥajar, Tahdhīb , vol. 6, p. 314." aria-label="Footnote 39: Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh , vol. 36, p. 163. Ibn Khallikān, Wafay">39</a></sup> he grew up in Yemen and studied there, but also undertook business trips to Syria which surely led him through Mecca and Medina, where he used the opportunity to meet with the scholars there.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn40" id="fnref40" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="40" data-fn-text="Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh , vol. 36, pp. 160, 162, 178. al-Dhahabī, Tadhkira , vol. 1, p. 364. al-Ṣafadī, Nakt , p. 191." aria-label="Footnote 40: Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh , vol. 36, pp. 160, 162, 178. al-Dhahab">40</a></sup> Later he lived and taught in Yemen and died there at the age of 85 years<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn41" id="fnref41" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="41" data-fn-text="al-Dhahabī, Tadhkira , vol. 1, p. 364. Less precisely: Ibn al-ʿImād, Shadharāt , vol. 2, p. 27." aria-label="Footnote 41: al-Dhahabī, Tadhkira , vol. 1, p. 364. Less precisely: Ibn a">41</a></sup> in the middle of the month of Shawwāl of the year 211/827.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn42" id="fnref42" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="42" data-fn-text="Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt , vol. 5, p. 399. al-Bukhārī, Taʾrīkh , vol. 3/2, p. 130. Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh , vol. 36, p. 192. Ibn Khallikān, Wafayāt , vol. 2, p. 371. Ibn al-Nadīm, Fihrist , p. 318. al-Dhahabī, Tadhkira , vol. 1, p. 364. Id., Mīzān , vol. 2, p. 126. p. 129. Ibn Kathīr, Bidāya , vol. 9, p. 265. Ibn Ḥajar, Tahdhīb , vol. 6, p. 314. Ibn al-ʿImād, Shadharāt , vol. 2, p. 27." aria-label="Footnote 42: Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt , vol. 5, p. 399. al-Bukhārī, Taʾrīkh , vo">42</a></sup>
            </p>

            <p>
              ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s most important teacher was Maʿmar ibn Rāshid, who originated from Basra but had settled in Yemen. According to his own statements, he studied with him for seven, eight or nine years.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn44" id="fnref44" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="44" data-fn-text="Ibn Abī Ḥātim, Jarḥ , vol. 3, p. 38 (source: Muḥammad ibn Abān al-Balkhī). Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh , vol. 36, p. 167 (source: Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal). al-Dhahabī, Tadhkira , vol. 1, p. 364. Id., Mīzān , vol. 2, p. 126 (Maʿmar should be read instead of ʿUmar)." aria-label="Footnote 44: Ibn Abī Ḥātim, Jarḥ , vol. 3, p. 38 (source: Muḥammad ibn Ab">44</a></sup> On the basis of his age, this must have been in the last years of the life of Maʿmar, who died in 153/770.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn43" id="fnref43" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="43" data-fn-text="Cf. Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt , vol. 5, p. 397. al-Bukhārī, Taʾrīkh , vol. 4, p. 378. Ibn Ḥibbān, Mashāhīr , no. 1543. Ibn Ḥajar, Tahdhīb , vol. 10, p. 243; vol. 6, p. 311." aria-label="Footnote 43: Cf. Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt , vol. 5, p. 397. al-Bukhārī, Taʾrīkh ">43</a></sup><sup>,</sup><sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn45" id="fnref45" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="45" data-fn-text="Variants: 152, 154." aria-label="Footnote 45: Variants: 152, 154.">45</a></sup> He was present at his death;<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn46" id="fnref46" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="46" data-fn-text="Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt , vol. 5, p. 397 (source: Ibn ʿUyayna from ʿAbd al-Razzāq himself)." aria-label="Footnote 46: Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt , vol. 5, p. 397 (source: Ibn ʿUyayna from">46</a></sup> presumably at that time he was still his student. The beginning of his studies with Maʿmar is thus to be dated approximately in his twentieth year.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn47" id="fnref47" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="47" data-fn-text="This is probably what is referred to by al-Dhahabī’s statement, Mīzān , vol. 2, p. 126, that he devoted himself to the study of Tradition ( ṭalaba l-ʿilm ) at the age of 20. On the relationship between ʿAbd al-Razzāq and Maʿmar cf. also Ibn Ḥajar, Tahdhīb , vol. 6, pp. 311, 312, 313." aria-label="Footnote 47: This is probably what is referred to by al-Dhahabī’s stateme">47</a></sup>
            </p>

            <p>Earlier, however, he seems to have taken advantage of a visit of the Meccan Ibn Jurayj in Yemen to attend his lectures.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn48" id="fnref48" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="48" data-fn-text="Cf. also Ibn Abī Ḥātim, Taqdima , pp. 52 f. Also Ibn Ḥajar, Tahdhīb , vol. 6, pp. 311, 312." aria-label="Footnote 48: Cf. also Ibn Abī Ḥātim, Taqdima , pp. 52 f. Also Ibn Ḥajar, ">48</a></sup> According to the statement of an older classmate of ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s,<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn49" id="fnref49" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="49" data-fn-text="On him cf. al-Dhahabī, Tadhkira , vol. 1, p. 346." aria-label="Footnote 49: On him cf. al-Dhahabī, Tadhkira , vol. 1, p. 346.">49</a></sup> the later muftī and qāḍī of Ṣanʿāʾ Hishām ibn Yūsuf (d. 197/812–3), he was then 18 years old,<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn50" id="fnref50" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="50" data-fn-text="Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh , vol. 36, p. 167. al-Dhahabī, Mīzān , vol. 2, p. 127." aria-label="Footnote 50: Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh , vol. 36, p. 167. al-Dhahabī, Mīzān , ">50</a></sup> that is, Ibn Jurayj’s trip to Yemen would have to have taken place in the year 144/761–2. That is quite possible, since Ibn Jurayj’s journeys in the last years of his life—he died in 150/767—are documented elsewhere as well,<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn51" id="fnref51" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="51" data-fn-text="See below, p. 282." aria-label="Footnote 51: See below, p. 282.">51</a></sup> and his presence is indicated in Basra in the following year.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn52" id="fnref52" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="52" data-fn-text="al-Bukhārī, Taʾrīkh , vol. 3/2, p. 130. Ibn Abī Ḥātim, Jarḥ , vol. 3, p. 39. al-Dhahabī, Tadhkira , vol. 1, p. 364. Id., Mīzān , vol. 2, p. 128. al-Ṣafadī, Nakt , p. 191." aria-label="Footnote 52: al-Bukhārī, Taʾrīkh , vol. 3/2, p. 130. Ibn Abī Ḥātim, Jarḥ ">52</a></sup> Sufyān al-Thawrī (d. 161/777–8) also numbers among ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s more significant teachers. He made a stay in Yemen in the year 149/766, and ʿAbd al-Razzāq probably obtained the bulk of the material transmitted from him on this occasion.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn53" id="fnref53" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="53" data-fn-text="Cf. Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt , vol. 5, p. 365 (biography of Ibn ʿUyayna; source: Ibn ʿUyayna). Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh , vol. 36, pp. 167, 168 (sources: ʿAbd al-Razzāq, Zayd ibn al-Mubārak). al-Dhahabī, Tadhkira , vol. 1, p. 346 (biography of Hishām ibn Yūsuf; source: Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsā). Ibn Ḥajar, Tahdhīb , vol. 6, pp. 311, 313." aria-label="Footnote 53: Cf. Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt , vol. 5, p. 365 (biography of Ibn ʿUy">53</a></sup>
            </p>

            <p>
              The same is true of Sufyān ibn ʿUyayna (d. 198/813–4), who visited Yemen in the years 150/767 and 152/769<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn54" id="fnref54" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="54" data-fn-text="Ibn Saʿd, as in note 53." aria-label="Footnote 54: Ibn Saʿd, as in note 53.">54</a></sup> and is named in the biographical literature as a teacher of ʿAbd al-Razzāq.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn55" id="fnref55" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="55" data-fn-text="Ibn Khallikān, Wafayāt , vol. 2, pp. 129, 371. al-Ṣafadī, Nakt , p. 191. Ibn Ḥajar, Tahdhīb , vol. 6, p. 311." aria-label="Footnote 55: Ibn Khallikān, Wafayāt , vol. 2, pp. 129, 371. al-Ṣafadī, Na">55</a></sup> That at this time he was already studying with Ibn ʿUyayna can be inferred from a remark of ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s that he presented a ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUyayna to Maʿmar.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn56" id="fnref56" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="56" data-fn-text="Ibn Abī Ḥātim, Taqdima , p. 52 (Source: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī, d. 265/878–9, a student of ʿAbd al-Razzāq. On him cf. al-Dhahabī, Tadhkira , vol. 2, pp. 364, 564 f. al-Ṣafadī, Nakt , p. 191)." aria-label="Footnote 56: Ibn Abī Ḥātim, Taqdima , p. 52 (Source: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-">56</a></sup> Furthermore, it is not impossible that ʿAbd al-Razzāq repeatedly contacted the Meccans Ibn Jurayj and Ibn ʿUyayna, as well as the Kufan al-Thawrī, who spent most of the years 155/772–160/777 in Mecca,<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn57" id="fnref57" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="57" data-fn-text="Cf. al-Baghdādī, Taʾrīkh , vol. 9, pp. 71, 153, 159 f." aria-label="Footnote 57: Cf. al-Baghdādī, Taʾrīkh , vol. 9, pp. 71, 153, 159 f.">57</a></sup> on the occasion of the <em>ḥajj</em>. Aside from the people named, further names of informants are listed in the biographical works, among them ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, al-Awzāʿī and Mālik, to name only the better known.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn58" id="fnref58" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="58" data-fn-text="Ibn Abī Ḥātim, Jarḥ , vol. 3, p. 38. Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh , vol. 36, p. 160, 165. Ibn Khallikān, Wafayāt , vol. 2, p. 371. al-Dhahabī, Tadhkira , vol. 1, p. 364. Id., Mīzān , vol. 2, pp. 126, 128. al-Ṣafadī, Nakt , p. 191. Ibn Ḥajar, Tahdhīb , vol. 6, p. 311." aria-label="Footnote 58: Ibn Abī Ḥātim, Jarḥ , vol. 3, p. 38. Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh , ">58</a></sup> Most can also be documented as such in the <em>Muṣannaf</em>.
            </p>

            <p>
              Thus, the statements of the biographical literature about ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s teachers to a large extent correspond to the information which can be gained from his work itself about his more significant sources. Since the available evidence does not indicate a direct dependence of the biographical reports on the work of ʿAbd al-Razzāq—in the form of their being extracted from it—is not to be observed, they may be regarded as an independent confirmation of the conclusions drawn from the work itself.
            </p>

            <p>
              ʿAbd al-Razzāq achieved such fame as a scholar in the last quarter of the second/eighth century that he attracted students from all corners of the Islamic oikoumene. Among them were the Iraqis Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal and Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn, two of the outstanding ʿulamāʾ of the first half of the third/ninth century, who studied with him for a year before the turn of the century.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn59" id="fnref59" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="59" data-fn-text="Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh , vol. 36, pp. 174, 176. Ibn Khallikān, Wafayāt , vol. 2, p. 371. al-Dhahabī, Mīzān , vol. 2, pp. 126, 127, 128. al-Ṣafadī, Nakt , p. 192. Ibn Ḥajar, Tahdhīb , vol. 6, pp. 311, 312, 313. Ibn al-ʿImād, Shadharāt , vol. 2, p. 27." aria-label="Footnote 59: Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh , vol. 36, pp. 174, 176. Ibn Khallikān,">59</a></sup> Also found among the numerous students of ʿAbd al-Razzāq is the name Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm al-Dabarī, from whom a large part of the version of the <em>Muṣannaf</em> which has come down to us derives.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn60" id="fnref60" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="60" data-fn-text="Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh , vol. 36, p. 161. al-Dhahabī, Tadhkira , vol. 1, p. 364. Id., Mīzān , vol. 2, p. 128. al-Ṣafadī, Nakt , p. 191 (al-Dayrī should be corrected to al-Dabarī). Ibn Ḥajar, Tahdhīb , vol. 6, p. 311. On him see below, pp. 68 f." aria-label="Footnote 60: Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh , vol. 36, p. 161. al-Dhahabī, Tadhkira">60</a></sup>
            </p>

            <p>
              ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s reputation rested above all on his book or books. The earliest references to them derive from his students Ibn Maʿīn (d. 233/847) and Ibn Ḥanbal (d. 241/855–6) and their student al-Bukhārī (d. 256/870).<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn61" id="fnref61" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="61" data-fn-text="Cf. al-Dhahabī, Mīzān , vol. 2, p. 127. al-Bukhārī, Taʾrīkh , vol. 3/2, p. 130. Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh , vol. 36, pp. 164, 181, 183." aria-label="Footnote 61: Cf. al-Dhahabī, Mīzān , vol. 2, p. 127. al-Bukhārī, Taʾrīkh ">61</a></sup> From them it can be inferred only that the books came into being before the turn of the century and that, among other things, they contained ḥadīths, but the references provide neither a title nor any details about their structure. Ibn al-Nadīm (d. 385/995) mentions a <em>Kitāb al-Sunan fī l-fiqh</em> and a <em>Kitāb al-Maghāzī</em> by him.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn62" id="fnref62" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="62" data-fn-text="Ibn al-Nadīm, Fihrist , p. 318." aria-label="Footnote 62: Ibn al-Nadīm, Fihrist , p. 318.">62</a></sup> The designation “sunan book” leads one to assume that it was a work of the <em>muṣannaf</em> type. This is also implied by a number of characterisations of his work from the fourth/tenth century and later: Ibn ʿAdī (d. 365/975–6) remarks of ʿAbd al-Razzāq that he possessed <em>aṣnāf</em> and a voluminous Ḥadīth.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn63" id="fnref63" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="63" data-fn-text="Ibn Ḥajar, Tahdhīb , vol. 6, pp. 313, 314." aria-label="Footnote 63: Ibn Ḥajar, Tahdhīb , vol. 6, pp. 313, 314.">63</a></sup> Ibn Ḥibbān (d. 354/965) numbers him among those who gathered (<em>jamaʿa</em>) and ordered thematically (<em>ṣannafa</em>). Al-Khushanī (d. 371/981–2) speaks of a “Kitāb ʿAbd al-Razzāq fī khtilāf al-nās fī l-fiqh.”<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn64" id="fnref64" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="64" data-fn-text="Cf. M. Muranyi, “Das Kitāb Musnad ḥadīṯ Mālik ibn Anas von Ismāʿīl b. Isḥāq al-Qāḍī (199/815–282/895),” Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft 138 (1988), p. 134." aria-label="Footnote 64: Cf. M. Muranyi, “Das Kitāb Musnad ḥadīṯ Mālik ibn Anas von I">64</a></sup> Ibn Khayr (d. 575/1179–80) knows the <em>Muṣannaf</em> by ʿAbd al-Razzāq in different <em>riwāya</em>s and mentions a <em>kitāb al-maghāzī</em> and a <em>kitāb al-jāmiʿ</em> as parts of it.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn65" id="fnref65" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="65" data-fn-text="Ibn Khayr, Fahrasa , pp. 127–130, 236. This reference was supplied by Maribel Fierro." aria-label="Footnote 65: Ibn Khayr, Fahrasa , pp. 127–130, 236. I owe this reference ">65</a></sup>
            </p>

            <p>al-Dhahabī (d. 748/1347–8) writes: <em>“Ṣannafa al-Jāmiʿ al-kabīr”</em> (he composed the Jāmiʿ al-kabīr arranged according to subject areas),<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn66" id="fnref66" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="66" data-fn-text="al-Dhahabī, Mīzān , vol. 2, p. 126." aria-label="Footnote 66: al-Dhahabī, Mīzān , vol. 2, p. 126.">66</a></sup> and in another place, “He was the author of al-Taṣānīf.”<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn67" id="fnref67" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="67" data-fn-text="Id., Tadhkira , vol. 1, p. 367." aria-label="Footnote 67: Id., Tadhkira , vol. 1, p. 367.">67</a></sup> al-Ṣafadī (d. 764/1363) has: <em>“Ṣannafa l-Tafsīr wa-l-Sunan.”</em><sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn68" id="fnref68" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="68" data-fn-text="al-Ṣafadī, Nakt , p. 192." aria-label="Footnote 68: al-Ṣafadī, Nakt , p. 192.">68</a></sup> This last indicates the existence of a Tafsīr transmitted from him.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn69" id="fnref69" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="69" data-fn-text="The Tafsīr has been edited recently several times: al-Riyāḍ, 1989; Beirut, 1991 and 1999. It is partially preserved in al-Ṭabarī’s Jāmiʿ . Cf. Horst, “Zur Überlieferung,” pp. 295, 297. Sezgin, Geschichte , vol. 1, p. 99." aria-label="Footnote 69: The Tafsīr has been edited recently several times: al-Riyāḍ,">69</a></sup> Ibn Kathīr (d. 774/1372–3) mentions him as the author of the <em>Muṣannaf</em> and of the <em>Musnad</em>,<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn70" id="fnref70" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="70" data-fn-text="Ibn Kathīr, Bidāya , vol. 9, p. 265. al-Dabbāgh (d. 696/1297) also has “Muṣannaf” in Maʿālim al-īmān , according to Muranyi, “Das Kitāb Musnad ḥadīṯ Mālik,” p. 134." aria-label="Footnote 70: Ibn Kathīr, Bidāya , vol. 9, p. 265. al-Dabbāgh (d. 696/1297">70</a></sup> al-Ṣafadī and Ibn al-ʿImād (d. 1089/1687) as the composer of the <em>Muṣannafāt</em>.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn71" id="fnref71" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="71" data-fn-text="al-Ṣafadī, Wāfī , vol. 6, p. 394. Ibn al-ʿImād, Shadharāt , vol. 2, p. 27." aria-label="Footnote 71: al-Ṣafadī, Wāfī , vol. 6, p. 394. Ibn al-ʿImād, Shadharāt , ">71</a></sup> It is to be assumed that the <em>Kitāb al-sunan fī l-fiqh</em>, the <em>Jāmiʿ al-kabīr</em>, the <em>Taṣānīf</em>, the <em>Muṣannaf</em> and the <em>Muṣannafāt</em> are one and the same work, of which the present edition of the manuscripts entitled <em>al-Muṣannaf</em> represents a recension.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn72" id="fnref72" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="72" data-fn-text="Ḥājjī Khalīfa asserts the identity of ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s Muṣannaf and his Jāmiʿ fī l-ḥadīth , cf. Kashf , vol. 2, col. 1712 (cf. vol. 1, col. 576)." aria-label="Footnote 72: Ḥājjī Khalīfa asserts the identity of ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s Muṣa">72</a></sup><sup>,</sup><sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn73" id="fnref73" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="73" data-fn-text="It would be wrong, however, to conclude, as G. R. Hawting did in his review in Bulletin of the School of Oriental and African Studies 59 (1996), p. 142, that early scholars did not associate a work called al-Muṣannaf with ʿAbd al-Razzāq simply because the title “al-Muṣannaf” appears only late in Islamic biographical literature. ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s Muṣannaf was known by this title to Ibn Mufarrij (d. 380/990–1), i.e. in the fourth/tenth century al-Andalus (cf. Ibn Khayr, Fahrasa (Saragossa, 1894), pp. 128–130), and—obviously independently from that transmission—to Ibn Abī Zayd al-Qayrawānī (d. 386/996) (cf. M. Muranyi, Beiträge zur Geschichte der Ḥadīth- und Rechtsgelehrsamkeit der Mālikiyya in Nordafrika bis zum 5. Jh. d. H. (Wiesbaden, 1997), p. 256). Muranyi also mentions (p. 206) that al-Dabarī’s transmission had already been brought to Qayrawān a generation earlier by Ibn Abī l-Manẓūr (d. 337/948) under the title Kitāb ʿAbd al-Razzāq fī khtilāf al-nās fī l-fiqh . The fact that the work was transmitted with different titles almost from the beginning does not necessarily mean that the work achieved its literary stabilization only much later, as Hawting suggests (op. cit., p. 143). If the work is not an authored book but—as I think—the transcription of ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s lectures in which he transmitted his thematically arranged collections of legally relevant traditions, it is easily understandable that the whole had no title given to it by ʿAbd al-Razzāq himself. The lack of title does not mean, however, that there was no work by him at all or that it was very different from that presented in the manuscripts written in the seventh/thirteenth and eighth/fourteenth centuries." aria-label="Footnote 73: It would be wrong, however, to conclude, as G. R. Hawting di">73</a></sup>
            </p>

            <p>
              However, ʿAbd al-Razzāq was already controversial in his lifetime. Several reasons for this can be discerned:
            </p>

            <div class="monograph-controversy-list">
              <div class="controversy-item">
                <span class="controversy-num">1.</span>
                <div class="controversy-content">
                  <strong>Inaccuracies in oral transmission vs. written precision:</strong> It is true that it is emphasized by his students that he knew the Ḥadīth of Maʿmar by heart and was better versed in this area than other students of Maʿmar,<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn74" id="fnref74" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="74" data-fn-text="al-Dhahabī, Tadhkira , vol. 1, p. 364. Id., Mīzān , vol. 2, p. 127 (source: Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal). Ibn Abī Ḥātim, Jarḥ , vol. 3, p. 38 (source: Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn), 39 (source: Abū Zurʿa). Ibn Ḥajar, Tahdhīb , vol. 6, p. 312." aria-label="Footnote 74: al-Dhahabī, Tadhkira , vol. 1, p. 364. Id., Mīzān , vol. 2, ">74</a></sup> that his transmission from Ibn Jurayj was more reliable than that of others,<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn75" id="fnref75" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="75" data-fn-text="al-Dhahabī, Mīzān , vol. 2, p. 127 (source: Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal). al-Ṣafadī, Nakt , p. 191. Ibn Ḥajar, Tahdhīb , vol. 6, p. 312." aria-label="Footnote 75: al-Dhahabī, Mīzān , vol. 2, p. 127 (source: Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal">75</a></sup> and that the material of his book consisted exclusively of direct, “heard” traditions,<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn76" id="fnref76" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="76" data-fn-text="Op. cit. (source: Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn)." aria-label="Footnote 76: Op. cit. (source: Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn).">76</a></sup> but Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn and Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal were able to observe that he made mistakes when he was not reading from his written texts.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn77" id="fnref77" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="77" data-fn-text="al-Ṣafadī, Nakt , p. 192 (source: Abū Khaythama Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb)." aria-label="Footnote 77: al-Ṣafadī, Nakt , p. 192 (source: Abū Khaythama Zuhayr ibn Ḥ">77</a></sup> It was also reported of him that he once let himself be prevailed upon to read aloud ḥadīths written by others that were unknown to him, which was regarded as passing on materials one had not heard oneself and was strongly condemned by the critical scholars.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn78" id="fnref78" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="78" data-fn-text="Ibn Abī Ḥātim, Jarḥ , vol. 3, p. 39 (source: Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn from Abū Jaʿfar al-Suwaydī). On a similar case see Goldziher, Muslim Studies , vol. 2, p. 176." aria-label="Footnote 78: Ibn Abī Ḥātim, Jarḥ , vol. 3, p. 39 (source: Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn">78</a></sup> Because of this, Yaḥyā refused to write down traditions from ʿAbd al-Razzāq which were not recorded in his “book.”<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn79" id="fnref79" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="79" data-fn-text="al-Dhahabī, Mīzān , vol. 2, p. 127. al-Ṣafadī, Nakt , p. 192." aria-label="Footnote 79: al-Dhahabī, Mīzān , vol. 2, p. 127. al-Ṣafadī, Nakt , p. 192">79</a></sup> Al-Bukhārī followed him in this, and considered as “ṣaḥīḥ” only the traditions contained “in his book.”<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn80" id="fnref80" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="80" data-fn-text="al-Bukhārī, Taʾrīkh , vol. 3/2, p. 130." aria-label="Footnote 80: al-Bukhārī, Taʾrīkh , vol. 3/2, p. 130.">80</a></sup>
                </div>
              </div>

              <div class="controversy-item">
                <span class="controversy-num">2.</span>
                <div class="controversy-content">
                  <strong>Blindness in old age and student collations:</strong> In the last years of his life ʿAbd al-Razzāq lost his eyesight and could not himself check against the original copies of his book presented to him,<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn81" id="fnref81" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="81" data-fn-text="al-Dhahabī, Mīzān , vol. 2, p. 127 (source: Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal). al-Ṣafadī, Nakt , p. 191; Ibn Ḥajar, Tahdhīb , vol. 6, p. 312." aria-label="Footnote 81: al-Dhahabī, Mīzān , vol. 2, p. 127 (source: Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal">81</a></sup> but depended in cases of doubt on the versions of the students whom he knew to be particularly accurate,<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn82" id="fnref82" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="82" data-fn-text="al-Baghdādī, Kifāya , p. 259 (source: Isḥāq ibn Abī Isrāʾīl, i.e., Abū Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm al-Marwazī, d. 245/859–60, a student of ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s. On him cf. al-Dhahabī, Tadhkira , vol. 2, pp. 484 f.)." aria-label="Footnote 82: al-Baghdādī, Kifāya , p. 259 (source: Isḥāq ibn Abī Isrāʾīl,">82</a></sup> a procedure which he had perhaps also practiced before becoming blind. Furthermore, he is supposed to have dictated texts from memory.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn83" id="fnref83" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="83" data-fn-text="al-Dhahabī, Mīzān , vol. 2, p. 127. al-Ṣafadī, Nakt , p. 191. Ibn Ḥajar, Tahdhīb , vol. 6, p. 312." aria-label="Footnote 83: al-Dhahabī, Mīzān , vol. 2, p. 127. al-Ṣafadī, Nakt , p. 191">83</a></sup> Because of this, Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal deemed the traditions of people who studied with him in this period to be <em>ḍaʿīf</em> (unreliable).<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn84" id="fnref84" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="84" data-fn-text="al-Dhahabī, Mīzān , vol. 2, p. 128." aria-label="Footnote 84: al-Dhahabī, Mīzān , vol. 2, p. 128.">84</a></sup> Later scholars such as Ibn al-Ṣalāḥ (d. 643/1245–6) joined him in this opinion and—following al-Nasāʾī (d. 303/915–6)—insisted that texts deriving from ʿAbd al-Razzāq be tested.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn85" id="fnref85" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="85" data-fn-text="al-Ṣafadī, Nakt , p. 192. al-Dhahabī, op. cit. Ibn Ḥajar, Tahdhīb , vol. 6, p. 314." aria-label="Footnote 85: al-Ṣafadī, Nakt , p. 192. al-Dhahabī, op. cit. Ibn Ḥajar, Ta">85</a></sup>
                </div>
              </div>

              <div class="controversy-item">
                <span class="controversy-num">3.</span>
                <div class="controversy-content">
                  <strong>Sympathy for moderate Shīʿism:</strong> Such fundamental reservations were based less on ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s transmission practices than on his sympathy for the Shīʿa.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn86" id="fnref86" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="86" data-fn-text="Cf. Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh , vol. 36, pp. 186, 187. al-Dhahabī, Mīzān , vol. 2, pp. 127–128. al-Ṣafadī, Nakt , pp. 191–192. Ibn Ḥajar, Tahdhīb , vol. 6, p. 313." aria-label="Footnote 86: Cf. Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh , vol. 36, pp. 186, 187. al-Dhahabī">86</a></sup> It is attested by his profession to Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn and by numerous pro-ʿAlid statements. ʿAbd al-Razzāq was won for the Shīʿa—clearly only at a rather advanced age—by Jaʿfar ibn Sulaymān al-Ḍubaʿī (d. 178/794–5) during the latter's sojourn in the Yemen.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn87" id="fnref87" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="87" data-fn-text="Op. cit. in note 86. On Jaʿfar ibn Sulaymān cf. al-Dhahabī, Tadhkira , vol. 1, p. 241." aria-label="Footnote 87: Op. cit. in note 86. On Jaʿfar ibn Sulaymān cf. al-Dhahabī, ">87</a></sup> Some of his students deserted him for this reason,<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn88" id="fnref88" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="88" data-fn-text="Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh , vol. 36, p. 187, 188. al-Dhahabī, Mīzān , vol. 2, p. 127." aria-label="Footnote 88: Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh , vol. 36, p. 187, 188. al-Dhahabī, Mīz">88</a></sup> but Hadīth specialists such as Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn and Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal did not regard his transmission as devalued by it. The statement is reported from Yaḥyā: <em>“Even if ʿAbd al-Razzāq were to lapse from Islam, we would not give up his Hadīth.”</em><sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn89" id="fnref89" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="89" data-fn-text="Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh , vol. 36, p. 192. al-Dhahabī, Mīzān , vol. 2, p. 128. Ibn Ḥajar, Tahdhīb , vol. 6, p. 314. On Aḥmad’s opinion cf. also Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh , vol. 36, p. 186. al-Dhahabī, op. cit., pp. 127, 129; Ibn Ḥajar, op. cit., pp. 311, 313." aria-label="Footnote 89: Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh , vol. 36, p. 192. al-Dhahabī, Mīzān , ">89</a></sup> His Shīʿism is generally described as moderate.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn90" id="fnref90" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="90" data-fn-text="Op. cit. (source: Aḥmad). al-Dhahabī, Tadhkira , vol. 1, p. 364." aria-label="Footnote 90: Op. cit. (source: Aḥmad). al-Dhahabī, Tadhkira , vol. 1, p. ">90</a></sup> He is supposed to have distanced himself from more radical movements like that of the Rawāfiḍ.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn91" id="fnref91" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="91" data-fn-text="Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh , vol. 36, p. 191. al-Dhahabī, Mīzān , vol. 2, p. 128 (source: Abū Bakr ibn Zanjawayh). On the Rawāfiḍ cf. W. M. Watt/M. Marmura, Der Islam. II Politische Entwicklung und theologische Konzepte (Stuttgart 1985), pp. 159–164 and passim." aria-label="Footnote 91: Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh , vol. 36, p. 191. al-Dhahabī, Mīzān , ">91</a></sup> Nevertheless, some later scholars apparently took his conversion to the Shīʿa as an occasion to put his reliability in question. According to Abū Ḥātim (d. 277/890–1), for instance, one may indeed write down ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s Hadīth, but not depend on it.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn92" id="fnref92" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="92" data-fn-text="Ibn Abī Ḥātim, Jarḥ , vol. 3, p. 39. Cf. also Ibn ʿAsākir, Taʾrīkh , vol. 36, p. 172. Ibn Ḥajar, Tahdhīb , vol. 6, p. 314 (here the “lā” before “yuḥtajju bihi” has clearly been dropped)." aria-label="Footnote 92: Ibn Abī Ḥātim, Jarḥ , vol. 3, p. 39. Cf. also Ibn ʿAsākir, T">92</a></sup> Others, such as al-Bukhārī (d. 256/870), al-Dhuhlī (d. 258/872), al-ʿIjlī (d. 261/874–5), Abū Dāwūd (d. 275/888–9), al-Bazzār (d. 292/905), and al-Dāraquṭnī (d. 385/995), considered him, aside from exceptional cases, to be reliable.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn93" id="fnref93" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="93" data-fn-text="al-Bukhārī, Taʾrīkh , vol. 3/2, p. 130. al-Dhahabī, Tadhkira , vol. 1, p. 364. Id., Mīzān , vol. 2, p. 127. Ibn Ḥajar, Tahdhīb , vol. 6, p. 314. Ibn al-ʿImād, Shadharāt , vol. 2, p. 27." aria-label="Footnote 93: al-Bukhārī, Taʾrīkh , vol. 3/2, p. 130. al-Dhahabī, Tadhkira">93</a></sup>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h3 id="al-dabari-transmission" class="sub-heading">
              <span>The Transmission of Isḥāq b. Ibrāhīm al-Dabarī</span>
            </h3>

            <p>The edition of the <em>Muṣannaf</em> is based mainly on the version of the work transmitted by Abū Yaʿqūb Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbbād al-Dabarī.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn94" id="fnref94" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="94" data-fn-text="See p. 57." aria-label="Footnote 94: See p. 57.">94</a></sup> Not very much can be learned about him from the biographical literature.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn95" id="fnref95" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="95" data-fn-text="Cf. al-Dhahabī, Mīzān , vol. 1, p. 58. Id., ʿIbar , vol. 2, p. 74. Ibn Ḥajar, Lisān , vol. 1, pp. 349–350. al-Samʿānī, Ansāb , vol. 5, p. 304. al-Ṣafadī, Wāfī , vol. 6, p. 394 f. Ibn al-ʿImād, Shadharāt , vol. 2, p. 190." aria-label="Footnote 95: Cf. al-Dhahabī, Mīzān , vol. 1, p. 58. Id., ʿIbar , vol. 2, ">95</a></sup> He came from the village of Dabar near to Ṣanʿāʾ and already attended ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s lectures as a small boy with his father. He “heard” the <em>Muṣannaf</em> in 210/825–6—thus, a year before ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s death—under the supervision of his father (<em>bi-ʿtināʾ wālidihi</em>), at the age of approximately six years.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn97" id="fnref97" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="97" data-fn-text="Ibn Khayr, Fahrasa , p. 130. al-Baghdādī, Kifāya , p. 64. al-Dhahabī, Mīzān , vol. 1, p. 58 (in the Beirut edition no. 731). One source for this information is al-Dabarī’s Iraqi contemporary Ibrāhīm al-Ḥarbī (d. 285/898). On him cf. al-Dhahabī, Tadhkira , vol. 2, pp. 584 f." aria-label="Footnote 97: Ibn Khayr, Fahrasa , p. 130. al-Baghdādī, Kifāya , p. 64. al">97</a></sup> His father was thus a student of ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s, and it is to be assumed that he produced the manuscript which later passed into the possession of his son. Since, however, Isḥāq “heard” the text as a child or at least claimed to have done so, he was able to omit his father from the isnād. Isḥāq al-Dabarī is characterized as <em>“ṣaḥīḥ al-samāʿ”</em> (impeccable in oral transmission) and <em>“ṣadūq”</em> (upright),<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn98" id="fnref98" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="98" data-fn-text="The precise meaning of this and other termini of evaluation in Ḥadīth criticism is difficult to define and probably varies from author to author. Cf. Juynboll, Muslim Tradition , pp. 184 ff." aria-label="Footnote 98: The precise meaning of this and other termini of evaluation ">98</a></sup> but al-Dhahabī notes that he also transmitted unacceptable (<em>munkara</em>) ḥadīths from ʿAbd al-Razzāq, of which it was doubtful whether they really derived from ʿAbd al-Razzāq because they were unique, and texts about the authenticity of which his teacher was himself unsure. Muslim (d. 261/874–5), Abū ʿAwāna (d. 316/928–9), al-Ṭabarānī (d. 360/971), al-Dāraquṭnī (d. 385/995) and others, however, considered him reliable and drew ʿAbd al-Razzāq material from him. The suspicion that he belonged to the Shīʿa seems to feed exclusively on the fact that he was a student of ʿAbd al-Razzāq and transmitted some of his pro-ʿAlid statements.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn99" id="fnref99" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="99" data-fn-text="Muḥsin al-Amīn, Aʿyān al-Shīʿa , vol. 11, p. 35." aria-label="Footnote 99: Muḥsin al-Amīn, Aʿyān al-Shīʿa , vol. 11, p. 35.">99</a></sup> He died in 286/899.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn100" id="fnref100" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="100" data-fn-text="Cf. Ibn Khayr, Fahrasa , p. 130. al-Dhahabī's date of 182 is an error." aria-label="Footnote 100: Cf. Ibn Khayr, Fahrasa , p. 130. al-Dhahabī's date of 182 is">100</a></sup>
            </p>

            <p>
              According to the criteria of critical Ḥadīth scholars of the third/ninth century of the stature of an Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal, the transmission of ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s <em>Muṣannaf</em> through Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm should be categorized as worthless. It took place in the last years of his life, when he had become blind and was no longer able to check what was read to him with the necessary exactitude. The “heard” acquisition of the text by a six-year-old—even with the help of an adult—certainly does not contribute to a more positive evaluation.
            </p>

            <p>
              The historian must not necessarily adopt the strict standards of Ḥadīth criticism. For his purpose, a purely written, not “heard” textual transmission is completely usable, even at the risk that it is faultier. It is to be inferred from a comment of ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s student Isḥāq ibn Abī Isrāʾīl that at lectures several students simultaneously checked over their copies and when differences occurred the master clarified the valid version of the text. Despite his blindness, through this procedure a high degree of agreement between original and copy could be achieved.
            </p>

            <p>
              Since it is to be assumed that Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm al-Dabarī’s manuscript was prepared by his father or someone else from a text of ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s intended for instruction, Isḥāq’s age has no significance for the written process of transmission. Since the evidence indicates that neither Isḥāq nor later transmitters substantially expanded or changed the text—aside from minimal clarifications<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn101" id="fnref101" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="101" data-fn-text="Cf., for instance, AM 7: 12120, 13423, 13855." aria-label="Footnote 101: Cf., for instance, AM 7: 12120, 13423, 13855.">101</a></sup>—, it is to be assumed that Isḥāq’s tradition is an authentic version of the works of ʿAbd al-Razzāq.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn102" id="fnref102" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="102" data-fn-text="Specifically, the last version taught in his circle during his lifetime. The possibility cannot be precluded that ʿAbd al-Razzāq supplemented or abbreviated his collection several times in the course of his life." aria-label="Footnote 102: Specifically, the last version taught in his circle during h">102</a></sup> The fact that Isḥāq was still a child at the time of ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s death could also speak for the assumption that the text remained largely in its original form and was not supplemented with oral traditions to any great extent.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn103" id="fnref103" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="103" data-fn-text="Hawting objected to such a reconstruction that it was based on an “undynamic view of the tradition” and that “the effects of the continuous reworking of the tradition, the introduction of glosses and improvements, the abbreviation and expansion of material” and so forth, “let alone simple errors of scribes and narrators” do not allow one to speak of authentic material. This objection has three shortcomings: 1) It is not true that Motzki neglects those “effects,” as can be seen, for example, in the discussion of the corpora of traditions ascribed to Ibn Jurayj and Ibn ʿUyayna, both allegedly going back to ʿAmr ibn Dīnār (see below pp. 180–185) and in Motzki’s articles “Der Fiqh des [al-]Zuhrī,” “Quo vadis Ḥadīṯ-Forschung,” “The Prophet and the Cat,” and “The Murder of Ibn Abī l-Ḥuqayq.” 2) The possibility that traditions changed during the transmission process must not lead us to conclude that we must give up the idea of reconstructing their original form and documenting the changes. 3) The concept of “continuous reworking of the tradition” which includes all possible changes is too general to be of any practical use. We must differentiate between types of changes that occur during the transmission process. It is one of the results of this study that ʿAbd al-Razzāq and Ibn Jurayj can be characterized as collectors who tried to reproduce as accurately as possible the material which they had collected. This result does not exclude the possibility that they sometimes made mistakes and that later transmitters, copyists and even the modern editor of the work also made mistakes. Motzki indicated obvious errors where he encountered them. But these types of changes do not justify the conclusion that, for example, ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s transmission from Ibn Jurayj is not authentic as a whole, i.e. that we cannot be sure that the texts really go back to him and that they are generally so heavily distorted that they cannot be ascribed to Ibn Jurayj anymore. There are no indications of such dramatic changes. Hawting’s comparison with historical traditions is misleading. The free use of traditions in this genre may not be generalized and transferred to the field of legal Ḥadīth even if instances of manipulation can be observed here as well." aria-label="Footnote 103: Hawting objected to such a reconstruction that it was based ">103</a></sup>
            </p>

            <h3 id="hawting-rebuttal" class="sub-heading">
              <span>Rebuttal of G. R. Hawting’s Skepticism</span>
            </h3>

            <p>Hawting doubts the conclusion that the part of the text which is ascribed to al-Dabarī reproduces ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s teaching (cf. his review, p. 142). He claims that the text “should be seen as the work of a later generation.” His arguments are:
            </p>

            <ol class="monograph-nested-list">
              <li>“Reports in the sources indicate” that expressions such as <em>“qaraʾnā ʿalā”</em> are “often perfunctory”, i.e. are not an indication of direct transmission;</li>
              <li>Most of the traditions in the work begin with <em>“qāla ʿAbd al-Razzāq.”</em></li>
            </ol>

            <p>
              Neither argument is convincing:
            </p>

            <p>
              <strong>1)</strong> Reports (Which reports? In which sources?) that the expression <em>qaraʾnā ʿalā</em> was used although the text had not been read to the transmitter or author, cannot be generalized. It is dangerous to conclude on the basis of single reports that this happened “often” or almost always and that the term, therefore, has no specific meaning at all.
            </p>

            <p>
              <strong>2)</strong> The claim that “most of the traditions in the work begin with <em>qāla ʿAbd al-Razzāq</em>” is simply not correct. Al-Dabarī’s <em>riwāya</em> is usually introduced at the top of a <em>kitāb</em>, rarely at the beginning of a chapter, with <em>qaraʾnā ʿalā</em> and then mostly confines itself to giving only the name ʿAbd al-Razzāq at the head of the isnāds. Only three books of al-Dabarī’s transmission have <em>“akhbaranā ʿAbd al-Razzāq”</em> (vol. 2, p. 335, vol. 9, p. 199 and vol. 10, p. 379) and only two books (vol. 2, p. 271 and vol. 9, p. 137) have <em>“ʿan ʿAbd al-Razzāq”</em> in their introductory formulae. This system is frequently interrupted by the expression <em>“akhbaranā ʿAbd al-Razzāq”</em> which obviously means the same as <em>“qaraʾnā ʿalā ʿAbd al-Razzāq.”</em>
            </p>

            <p>
              In al-Qurṭubī’s <em>riwāya</em> the words <em>“akhbaranā ʿAbd al-Razzāq”</em> are even regularly used to introduce the isnāds. Two other transmissions from al-Dabarī, those of al-Aʿrābī and al-Būsī, correspond, however, in not giving <em>akhbaranā</em> every time. Therefore, their method of quotation seems to be al-Dabarī’s original text which has been systematically corrected by al-Qurṭubī. The method of quotation displayed in the (original) transmission of al-Dabarī’s text does not necessarily indicate later editing but this method may, of course, have been used by pupils when making copies of the material collected from their teacher.
            </p>

            <p>
              In comparison, very few texts begin with <em>“qāla ʿAbd al-Razzāq”</em> and they are clearly in most cases additions by ʿAbd al-Razzāq himself to traditions quoted before or they are his comments on them. Strikingly, many of the additional traditions introduced with <em>“qāla ʿAbd al-Razzāq”</em> continue with <em>“samiʿtu X”</em> or another expression of <em>samāʿ</em> which is normally not the case with the other types of introduction. It is erroneous to assume that the expression <em>“qāla X”</em> necessarily indicates that the text is of a later generation. It may also be a comment made by the author or transmitter of a work during the transmission process written down by the students in the margin of their copies and later integrated in the body of the text, as seems to be the case in al-Dabarī’s transmission of ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s <em>Muṣannaf</em>.
            </p>
          </section>
          <section id="section-c" class="monograph-section">
            <h2 class="section-heading">
              <span class="heading-roman">V.</span>
              <span class="heading-title">C. The Muṣannaf as a Source for Early Second-Century Legal History</span>
            </h2>

            <p>Thus the preceding analysis has clarified two prerequisites on which the utility of the work as a historical source ultimately depends:
            </p>

            <div class="monograph-prerequisite-block">
              <div class="prereq-item">
                <span class="prereq-index">1.</span>
                <div class="prereq-content">
                  <strong>Faithful preservation of teaching material:</strong> The recension available in an edited form very probably reproduces faithfully ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s teaching material—aside from the sequence of all the books, textual losses and errors which crept in during copying and editing. In other words, the <em>Muṣannaf</em> represents a text which is in principle trustworthy and whose origins can be dated in the first decade of the third/ninth century—perhaps even earlier.
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="prereq-item">
                <span class="prereq-index">2.</span>
                <div class="prereq-content">
                  <strong>Compilation of older early second-century sources:</strong> The work itself seems to be a compilation of the texts of older sources of varying size. They can be reconstructed from the statements of provenance (isnāds).<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn104" id="fnref104" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="104" data-fn-text="With “reconstruction of sources” Motzki did not mean here that we can reconstruct earlier works in their original form, but only that we can compile all the texts which are ascribed by ʿAbd al-Razzāq to main teachers." aria-label="Footnote 104: With “reconstruction of sources” Motzki did not mean here that we ">104</a></sup> ʿAbd al-Razzāq came into possession of the materials of his four main sources largely between the years 144/761–2 and 153/770. They are presumably texts which go back to scholars of the first half of the second/eighth century—only Ibn ʿUyayna lived much longer—, which the author acquired directly from them. Consequently, the materials of ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s main sources originated in the course of the first half of the second/eighth century and are thus among the earliest legally relevant textual collections of large dimensions which have appeared to date and whose authenticity can be considered ensured.<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn105" id="fnref105" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="105" data-fn-text="The word “authenticity” used here must not be misunderstood. Motzki did not mean that the content of the traditions ascribed by ʿAbd al-Razzāq to Ibn Jurayj, for example, is reliable, but only that his ascription to Ibn Jurayj can be trusted. Whether the material transmitted by Ibn Jurayj is reliable or not is another issue. Besides, Motzki’s judgement that the corpus of Ibn Jurayj traditions is authentic is limited to the material contained in ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s Muṣannaf . Texts ascribed to Ibn Jurayj in other sources are not included. The question as to whether his name was used by someone else to confer legitimacy cannot be answered without a detailed study of the sources in question. In the case of ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s Muṣannaf , however, it can be ruled out that Ibn Jurayj’s name was used by someone else." aria-label="Footnote 105: The word “authenticity” used here must not be misunderstood.">105</a></sup>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h3 id="juynboll-rebuttal" class="sub-heading">
              <span>Rebuttal of G. H. A. Juynboll’s Hyper-Skepticism</span>
            </h3>

            <p>G. H. A. Juynboll expressed some reservation about Motzki’s conclusion that the texts which in the <em>Muṣannaf</em> are ascribed to ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s main informants Ibn Jurayj, Maʿmar and al-Thawrī really derive from them (cf. his “New Perspectives in the Study of Early Islamic Jurisprudence?”, <em>Bibliotheca Orientalis</em> 49 (1992), pp. 358–361). He considers it possible that ʿAbd al-Razzāq had fictitiously ascribed several or even many texts to his alleged informants. He argues that it was common among Ḥadīth scholars of the third/ninth century to invent additional isnāds and mutūn. As evidence Juynboll refers to the fact that collections of the third/ninth century and later contain many traditions ascribed to ʿAbd al-Razzāq, Mālik, Sufyān ibn ʿUyayna and al-Ṭayālisī that cannot be found in the collections preserved under their names. These traditions must, therefore, have been forged.
            </p>

            <p>
              This argument is not convincing, however, because it is improbable that these collections are complete records of their teachings. Juynboll thinks, furthermore, that the textual elements which Motzki interpreted as “criteria of authenticity” were introduced by ʿAbd al-Razzāq on purpose <em>“in the expectation that even a critical Ḥadīth student such as Motzki, living many, say twelve centuries later, might fall for this, being taken in by these frills and tassels as ‘hallmarks of authenticity.’”</em>
            </p>

            <p>
              This and Juynboll’s other highly speculative arguments as to why forgery of informants on a large scale may be “conceivable” need to be substantiated in order to be acceptable. In the meantime, the working hypothesis can reasonably be adopted that ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s main sources are not fictitious. Motzki concurs with Juynboll that it is desirable to check “diligently every single tradition supposedly transmitted by Ibn Jurayj to his alleged pupil,” by comparing it with similar traditions in all other sources available, in order to be certain whether it really goes back to Ibn Jurayj. Yet testing all traditions of the <em>Muṣannaf</em> ascribed to Ibn Jurayj, Maʿmar, al-Thawrī and others in this manner needs generations of scholars devoting their energies to that enterprise. Comparison of individual traditions of the <em>Muṣannaf</em> with parallels in other sources had, in Motzki’s study, not yielded a tradition which ʿAbd al-Razzāq or his transmitters purposely falsely ascribed to one of his main informants.
            </p>

            <p>Since all four of the scholars from whom ʿAbd al-Razzāq has the greater part of his material are also known as the authors of written works which have until now been considered lost—for instance, the <em>Kitāb al-Sunan</em> of Ibn Jurayj or the <em>Jāmiʿ al-kabīr</em> and <em>al-ṣaghīr</em> of Sufyān al-Thawrī<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn106" id="fnref106" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="106" data-fn-text="Cf. Ibn al-Nadīm, Fihrist , pp. 315, 316. According to him Ibn ʿUyayna did not have a book; one could only hear his lectures. This probably means that he did not supply a written text to be copied. However, works are ascribed to him, which consequently are probably notes by his students: a Tafsīr (thus op. cit., p. 316) and a Kitāb al-Jawāmiʿ fī l-sunan wa-l-abwāb (thus Abū Ṭālib al-Makkī, Qūt al-qulūb , vol. 1, p. 324. Cf. also Sezgin, Bukhârî'nin kaynakları , p. 42). On Maʿmar’s Jāmiʿ , see above, p. 57, and Sezgin, “Hadis musannefatının mebdei.”" aria-label="Footnote 106: Cf. Ibn al-Nadīm, Fihrist , pp. 315, 316. According to him I">106</a></sup>—, the question presents itself whether such works—received in lectures—are not completely or partially ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s sources. It is imaginable that he cannibalized them and reworked them into a new synthesis. This impression is unavoidable; whether it is tenable can only be decided after reconstruction and a detailed investigation of the individual strands of sources.
            </p>

            <h3 id="meccan-jurisprudence" class="sub-heading">
              <span>Meccan Jurisprudence & The Choice of the Nikāḥ/Ṭalāq Corpus</span>
            </h3>

            <p>
              Another important problem is what informational value these sources have for the question of the origins and development of Islamic jurisprudence, how old the material that they contain is, where it comes from, what characteristics it displays in terms of form and content, and what conclusions can be drawn from it with respect to the historical question under examination. To address these questions and to test whether they can be answered at all with the help of these sources, Motzki selected two of ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s textual traditions—those of Ibn Jurayj and Ibn ʿUyayna—for a pilot study.
            </p>

            <p>
              One reason for this choice is that both are Meccan scholars. Since we know as good as nothing about the development of jurisprudence in Mecca in the first/seventh and second/eighth centuries—conditions in Medina and Kufa have been much more thoroughly researched and depicted<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn107" id="fnref107" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="107" data-fn-text="Cf. Schacht, Origins , pp. 8–9." aria-label="Footnote 107: Cf. Schacht, Origins , pp. 8–9.">107</a></sup>—there is an opportunity to fill this gap with the help of the materials of the two figures named. Another, decisive factor was the observation of certain formal characteristics of the Ibn Jurayj source which seemed particularly favorable for the determination of the provenance and authenticity of the texts contained in it.
            </p>

            <p>In view of the predominantly homogeneous structure of the <em>Muṣannaf</em>, it would not have been very efficient to extend the study over the entire work. Despite the expenditure of several extra years, the conclusions would not have looked very different. For this reason, Motzki selected a sufficiently large textual basis—the books <em>al-nikāḥ</em> and <em>al-ṭalāq</em>,<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn108" id="fnref108" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="108" data-fn-text="This book also contains material on al-riḍāʿ , al-nafaqa , al-ḥadd , al-zinā and so forth, which in later works are often to be found in their own or in other chapters." aria-label="Footnote 108: This book also contains material on al-riḍāʿ , al-nafaqa , a">108</a></sup> that is, three quarters of the sixth and the entire seventh volume of the work—, but took pains to depict the results in a representative way.
            </p>

            <p>
              In principle, they are valid for the entire work with the exception of the appended <em>kitāb al-jāmiʿ</em> and the <em>kitāb al-maghāzī</em>,<sup class="fn-ref-wrap"><a href="#fn109" id="fnref109" class="fn-anchor" data-footnote-ref data-fn-num="109" data-fn-text="For a first investigation of this kitāb cf. N. van der Voort, Zoektocht naar de waarheid met behulp van het Kitāb al-Maghāzī in de Muṣannaf van ʿAbd ar-Razzāq b. Hammām as-Sanʿānī (gest. 211/827) , M.A. thesis (Nijmegen 1996) and id., “Het Kitāb al-maghāzī van ʿAbd ar-Razzāq b. Hammām al-Sanʿānī,” Sharqiyyāt 11/1 (1999), 15–31." aria-label="Footnote 109: For a first investigation of this kitāb cf. N. van der Voort">109</a></sup> which contain material from neither Ibn Jurayj nor Ibn ʿUyayna, and of the <em>kitāb al-buyūʿ</em>, where texts of Maʿmar and al-Thawrī dominate. This limitation, furthermore, was intended to contribute to the transparency and testability of the argumentation, which often leave something to be desired in Schacht’s work on the origins of Islamic law, which may in part explain his lasting success. Not least, Motzki’s prior familiarity with Islamic law of marriage and divorce also played a role in the choice of the extract and was helpful in the clarification of many difficult passages.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="computational-reassessment" class="monograph-section">
            <h2 class="section-heading">
              <span class="heading-roman">VII.</span>
              <span class="heading-title">Modern Computational Reassessment: Testing Motzki Across the Complete <em>Muṣannaf</em> (N = 21,109 Traditions)</span>
            </h2>

            <p class="has-dropcap">
              <span class="illuminated-dropcap" aria-hidden="true">T</span>he manual procedure developed by Motzki in 1991 can now be subjected to a corpus-wide test. The complete digitized <em>Muṣannaf</em> dataset contains <strong>21,109 traditions</strong>, allowing his source-profile argument to be evaluated across the full surviving digital corpus rather than within the original sample alone. A boundary audit further establishes that the textual span Motzki described as approximately 3,810 traditions corresponds to <strong>3,807 physical database records</strong> in the opening portion of the al-Aʿẓamī edition. The discrepancy is produced by duplicated and skipped hadith numbers in the printed sequence, not by a mismatch of textual scope.
            </p>

            <div class="monograph-profile-dossiers">
              <div class="profile-dossier">
                <h4 class="profile-dossier__title">Corpus Expansion</h4>
                <p>
                  Motzki’s manual sample: <strong>~3,810</strong> traditions, approximately 21% of the relevant corpus. Complete digital corpus: <strong>21,109</strong> traditions, with 21,077 records containing an identifiable teacher at <em>pos</em> = 1.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="profile-dossier">
                <h4 class="profile-dossier__title">Four Principal Sources</h4>
                <p>
                  Maʿmar, Ibn Jurayj, al-Thawrī, and Ibn ʿUyaynah account for <strong>17,536 traditions</strong>, or 83.07% of the complete corpus and 83.20% of records with an identifiable teacher.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="profile-dossier">
                <h4 class="profile-dossier__title">Informant Resolution</h4>
                <p>
                  Form A models <strong>780 named informants</strong>; Form B expands this to <strong>782 categories</strong> by adding structural categories for <em>PERSONAL_DICTUM</em> and <em>ANONYMOUS</em>.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="profile-dossier">
                <h4 class="profile-dossier__title">Historical Scope</h4>
                <p>
                  The computational test concerns the coherence of source attribution and transmission structure; it does not by itself convert source-level coherence into proof of the verbatim authenticity of every individual <em>matn</em>.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h3 class="sub-heading">
              <span>1. Scale Expansion and the Stability of the Four Main Informants</span>
            </h3>

            <p>
              Motzki’s 1991 sample yielded approximately 32% Maʿmar, 29% Ibn Jurayj, 22% Sufyān al-Thawrī, and 4% Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah. Across all 21,109 digital records, the hierarchy remains unchanged: Maʿmar is the largest source at <strong>7,717 traditions (36.6%)</strong>, followed by Ibn Jurayj with <strong>4,744 (22.5%)</strong>, al-Thawrī with <strong>4,160 (19.7%)</strong>, and Ibn ʿUyaynah with <strong>915 (4.3%)</strong>. The shift in proportions therefore represents expansion and redistribution within a stable source hierarchy rather than the disappearance of Motzki’s structural pattern.
            </p>

            <div class="monograph-table-wrapper" tabindex="0" aria-label="Table 7.1: Motzki sample versus full Muṣannaf source proportions">
              <div class="table-caption">
                <strong>Table 7.1:</strong> Motzki’s manual source proportions compared with the complete digitized <em>Muṣannaf</em>.
              </div>
