            </div>

            <p>
              The most striking micro-level replication concerns Ibn Jurayj’s dependence on ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ. Motzki’s manual profile assigned approximately <strong>39%</strong> of the Ibn Jurayj corpus to ʿAṭāʾ; the complete corpus yields <strong>41.23%</strong>, or 1,956 reports. This near correspondence indicates that the strongly monocentric character of Ibn Jurayj’s transmission profile is not an artifact of the original ritual sample but an enduring structural property of the full source complex.
            </p>

            <h3 class="sub-heading">
              <span>2. Quantifying the “Individual Face”: Statistical Distance and Near-Orthogonality</span>
            </h3>

            <p>
              Motzki’s qualitative proposition that “each source has a completely individual face” can be expressed formally by representing each source as a probability vector over its named informants and structural categories. Two complementary metrics are especially useful: Jensen–Shannon Divergence, which measures distributional distance, and cosine similarity, which measures the degree to which two vectors point in the same direction. The complete corpus produces a <strong>mean pairwise JSD of 0.6797</strong> and a <strong>mean cosine similarity of 0.1064</strong>. In practical terms, the four source complexes occupy strongly separated regions of informant space and are therefore <em>near-orthogonal</em> rather than interchangeable distributions.
            </p>

