                    <th scope="row" class="cell-label">Ibn Jurayj / Ibn ʿUyaynah</th>
                    <td class="cell-num">0.6567</td>
                    <td class="cell-num">0.1366</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="row" class="cell-label">al-Thawrī / Ibn ʿUyaynah</th>
                    <td class="cell-num">0.5973</td>
                    <td class="cell-num">0.1405</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="row-summary">
                    <th scope="row" class="cell-label">Mean Pairwise Distance</th>
                    <td class="cell-num"><strong>0.6797</strong></td>
                    <td class="cell-num"><strong>0.1064</strong></td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>

            <p>
              The strongest separation occurs between Ibn Jurayj and al-Thawrī, whose profiles yield a JSD of <strong>0.7613</strong> and cosine similarity of only <strong>0.0530</strong>. The comparatively lowest divergence is found between al-Thawrī and Ibn ʿUyaynah (JSD 0.5973; cosine 0.1405), which is historically intelligible given their shared access to Kufan traditionists. The pattern is therefore not simple statistical isolation: it is differentiated overlap structured by plausible scholarly geography.
            </p>

            <h3 class="sub-heading">
              <span>3. The Permutation Test: Testing Arbitrary Compiler Assignment</span>
            </h3>

            <p>
              A large observed divergence is not sufficient by itself. The decisive question is whether a comparably differentiated result would arise if the same traditions were assigned arbitrarily to the four source labels. The primary experiment therefore used <strong>1,000 book-preserving stratified permutations</strong>. In every iteration, source labels were shuffled only among traditions within the same legal book, preserving the topical composition and source count of each <em>kitāb</em>.
            </p>

            <div class="monograph-table-wrapper" tabindex="0" aria-label="Table 7.3: Permutation test results">
              <div class="table-caption">
                <strong>Table 7.3:</strong> Book-preserving stratified permutation experiment, N = 1,000.
              </div>
              <table class="monograph-academic-table">
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="col" class="cell-label">Statistic</th>
                    <th scope="col">Observed</th>
                    <th scope="col">Null Distribution</th>
                    <th scope="col">Result</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="row" class="cell-label">Mean Pairwise JSD</th>
                    <td class="cell-num"><strong>0.6797</strong></td>
                    <td class="cell-num">0.1215 ± 0.0032</td>
                    <td class="cell-num">0 / 1,000 exceedances</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="row" class="cell-label">Null Range</th>
                    <td class="cell-num">—</td>
                    <td class="cell-num">0.1118–0.1324</td>
                    <td class="cell-num">Observed value far outside range</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="row" class="cell-label">Descriptive Distance</th>
                    <td class="cell-num"><strong>Z ≈ 175.8</strong></td>
                    <td class="cell-num">—</td>
                    <td class="cell-num">Extreme separation from null</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="row-summary">
                    <th scope="row" class="cell-label">Empirical p-value</th>
                    <td class="cell-num"><strong>≈ 0.001</strong></td>
                    <td class="cell-num">0 / 1,000 exceedances</td>
                    <td class="cell-num">Arbitrary assignment decisively disfavored</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>

            <p>
              The observed divergence of 0.6797 therefore stands far above the permutation distribution centered at 0.1215. No one of the 1,000 book-preserving simulations generated a divergence equal to or greater than the observed value. The experiment decisively rejects the narrow hypothesis of arbitrary compiler-level assignment: ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s four source labels cannot be treated as statistically indifferent bins into which traditions were randomly distributed.
            </p>

            <div class="monograph-isnād-extract">
              <div class="extract-label">Epistemological boundary of the permutation result</div>
              <blockquote class="extract-body">
                The permutation test establishes source-assignment coherence at the compiler level. It does <strong>not</strong> establish a 0.1% probability that the traditions were fabricated, does not eliminate the possibility of pre-compiler back-projection or isnād manipulation, and does not by itself establish prophetic authenticity.
              </blockquote>
            </div>

            <h3 class="sub-heading">
              <span>4. Stability Across Legal Topics</span>
            </h3>

            <p>
              The corpus-wide result is not confined to ritual purity or prayer. Across <strong>49 legal books</strong>, within-source profile similarity remains high: Maʿmar 0.887, Ibn Jurayj 0.842, al-Thawrī 0.795, and Ibn ʿUyaynah 0.750. A held-out 80/20 book-level cross-validation produces cosine similarities of 0.985, 0.967, 0.934, and 0.903 respectively. The “individual face” thus persists as a cross-topic property extending from <em>Ṭahārah</em> and <em>Ṣalāh</em> into commercial law, marriage, penal law, and <em>maghāzī</em>.
            </p>

            <h3 class="sub-heading">
              <span>5. Comparative Corpus Check: Ibn Abī Shaybah and External Attestation</span>
            </h3>

            <p>
              The strongest comparative control comes from stepping outside ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s editorial environment. The external-attestation audit cross-references the source-informant pairings against <strong>32 independent hadith collections</strong>, including the rival <em>Muṣannaf</em> of Ibn Abī Shaybah. Across these collections the study identifies <strong>33,186 non-ʿAbd al-Razzāq-mediated parallel attestations</strong>. The significance of this comparison is not that Ibn Abī Shaybah should reproduce the same numerical concentration of teachers, but that distinctive pairings observed inside ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s corpus recur in transmission lines that bypass him altogether. The pattern is therefore not reducible to a single compiler’s retrospective labelling practice.
            </p>

            <p>
              This cross-corpus evidence strengthens Motzki’s central inference. A late compiler-level fabricator could, in principle, construct an internally coherent pattern inside one collection; it is considerably harder to explain why the same source-informant relations should recur across independent collections transmitted through separate lines. The external evidence consequently weighs against a systemic model in which ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s concentrated source structure is simply a late retrospective fiction, while still leaving open the need for individual <em>isnād</em>-cum-<em>matn</em> criticism.
            </p>

            <h3 class="sub-heading">
              <span>6. Epistemological Interpretation and Limits</span>
            </h3>

            <p>
              The computational reassessment therefore provides strong support for a layered historical conclusion. First, ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s four principal source complexes are statistically non-interchangeable and exhibit stable, biographically intelligible transmission profiles. Second, the profile structure persists beyond Motzki’s hand-selected sample and is reproduced with substantial stability across the complete digital corpus. Third, the permutation experiment makes arbitrary retrospective compiler assignment statistically untenable. Fourth, the 33,186 external attestations show that important source-informant pairings were not confined to the editorial environment of ʿAbd al-Razzāq.
            </p>

            <p>
              None of these findings, however, licenses a universal authenticity claim. Statistical differentiation verifies that the compiler drew upon <strong>genuine, distinct transmission strata</strong>—consistent with written or memorized notebooks and teaching corpora associated with major early second-century authorities—but it does not automatically prove the verbatim authenticity of every individual prophetic <em>matn</em>, every intermediary attribution, or every legal maxim embedded within those strata. As the contrasting ICMA cases demonstrate, macro-level source coherence and micro-level textual historicity are analytically distinct problems.
            </p>

            <p>
              The appropriate historiographical conclusion is consequently neither wholesale skepticism nor unqualified authentication. The full corpus materially strengthens Motzki’s argument that the <em>Muṣannaf</em> preserves recoverable early source traditions, while preserving the methodological necessity of case-by-case philological critique. Computational profile analysis can establish structure, differentiation, stability, and non-randomness; the historical status of an individual <em>matn</em> still requires comparison of transmission branches, wording, chronology, geographical circulation, manuscript evidence, and the broader isnād-cum-matn record.
            </p>
