              <li id="fn102">
                Specifically, the last version taught in his circle during his lifetime. The possibility cannot be precluded that ʿAbd al-Razzāq supplemented or abbreviated his collection several times in the course of his life.
                <a href="#fnref102" class="apparatus-backref" aria-label="Back to reference 102">↩</a>
              </li>
              <li id="fn103">
                Hawting objected to such a reconstruction that it was based on an “undynamic view of the tradition” and that “the effects of the continuous reworking of the tradition, the introduction of glosses and improvements, the abbreviation and expansion of material” and so forth, “let alone simple errors of scribes and narrators” do not allow one to speak of authentic material. This objection has three shortcomings: 1) It is not true that Motzki neglects those “effects,” as can be seen, for example, in the discussion of the corpora of traditions ascribed to Ibn Jurayj and Ibn ʿUyayna, both allegedly going back to ʿAmr ibn Dīnār (see below pp. 180–185) and in Motzki’s articles “Der Fiqh des [al-]Zuhrī,” “Quo vadis Ḥadīṯ-Forschung,” “The Prophet and the Cat,” and “The Murder of Ibn Abī l-Ḥuqayq.” 2) The possibility that traditions changed during the transmission process must not lead us to conclude that we must give up the idea of reconstructing their original form and documenting the changes. 3) The concept of “continuous reworking of the tradition” which includes all possible changes is too general to be of any practical use. We must differentiate between types of changes that occur during the transmission process. It is one of the results of this study that ʿAbd al-Razzāq and Ibn Jurayj can be characterized as collectors who tried to reproduce as accurately as possible the material which they had collected. This result does not exclude the possibility that they sometimes made mistakes and that later transmitters, copyists and even the modern editor of the work also made mistakes. Motzki indicated obvious errors where he encountered them. But these types of changes do not justify the conclusion that, for example, ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s transmission from Ibn Jurayj is not authentic as a whole, i.e. that we cannot be sure that the texts really go back to him and that they are generally so heavily distorted that they cannot be ascribed to Ibn Jurayj anymore. There are no indications of such dramatic changes. Hawting’s comparison with historical traditions is misleading. The free use of traditions in this genre may not be generalized and transferred to the field of legal Ḥadīth even if instances of manipulation can be observed here as well.
                <a href="#fnref103" class="apparatus-backref" aria-label="Back to reference 103">↩</a>
              </li>
              <li id="fn104">
                With “reconstruction of sources” Motzki did not mean here that we can reconstruct earlier works in their original form, but only that we can compile all the texts which are ascribed by ʿAbd al-Razzāq to main teachers.
                <a href="#fnref104" class="apparatus-backref" aria-label="Back to reference 104">↩</a>
              </li>
              <li id="fn105">
                The word “authenticity” used here must not be misunderstood. Motzki did not mean that the content of the traditions ascribed by ʿAbd al-Razzāq to Ibn Jurayj, for example, is reliable, but only that his ascription to Ibn Jurayj can be trusted. Whether the material transmitted by Ibn Jurayj is reliable or not is another issue. Besides, Motzki’s judgement that the corpus of Ibn Jurayj traditions is authentic is limited to the material contained in ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s <em>Muṣannaf</em>. Texts ascribed to Ibn Jurayj in other sources are not included. The question as to whether his name was used by someone else to confer legitimacy cannot be answered without a detailed study of the sources in question. In the case of ʿAbd al-Razzāq’s <em>Muṣannaf</em>, however, it can be ruled out that Ibn Jurayj’s name was used by someone else.
                <a href="#fnref105" class="apparatus-backref" aria-label="Back to reference 105">↩</a>
              </li>
              <li id="fn106">
                Cf. Ibn al-Nadīm, <em>Fihrist</em>, pp. 315, 316. According to him Ibn ʿUyayna did not have a book; one could only hear his lectures. This probably means that he did not supply a written text to be copied. However, works are ascribed to him, which consequently are probably notes by his students: a <em>Tafsīr</em> (thus op. cit., p. 316) and a <em>Kitāb al-Jawāmiʿ fī l-sunan wa-l-abwāb</em> (thus Abū Ṭālib al-Makkī, <em>Qūt al-qulūb</em>, vol. 1, p. 324. Cf. also Sezgin, <em>Bukhârî'nin kaynakları</em>, p. 42). On Maʿmar’s <em>Jāmiʿ</em>, see above, p. 57, and Sezgin, “Hadis musannefatının mebdei.”
                <a href="#fnref106" class="apparatus-backref" aria-label="Back to reference 106">↩</a>
              </li>
              <li id="fn107">
                Cf. Schacht, <em>Origins</em>, pp. 8–9.
                <a href="#fnref107" class="apparatus-backref" aria-label="Back to reference 107">↩</a>
              </li>
              <li id="fn108">
                This book also contains material on <em>al-riḍāʿ</em>, <em>al-nafaqa</em>, <em>al-ḥadd</em>, <em>al-zinā</em> and so forth, which in later works are often to be found in their own or in other chapters.
                <a href="#fnref108" class="apparatus-backref" aria-label="Back to reference 108">↩</a>
              </li>
              <li id="fn109">
                For a first investigation of this kitāb cf. N. van der Voort, <em>Zoektocht naar de waarheid met behulp van het Kitāb al-Maghāzī in de Muṣannaf van ʿAbd ar-Razzāq b. Hammām as-Sanʿānī (gest. 211/827)</em>, M.A. thesis (Nijmegen 1996) and id., “Het Kitāb al-maghāzī van ʿAbd ar-Razzāq b. Hammām al-Sanʿānī,” <em>Sharqiyyāt</em> 11/1 (1999), 15–31.
                <a href="#fnref109" class="apparatus-backref" aria-label="Back to reference 109">↩</a>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </section>
        </article>

